<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zumaque I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zumaque I was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mene-grande">Mene Grande</a>, on the eastern side of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a>, that in 1914 became the first commercially productive well in Venezuela. Drilled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> Petroleum Company, an affiliate of Royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Shell, it marked the start of large-scale oil production in the country and is regarded as the birthplace of the Venezuelan oil industry.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The area between San Timoteo and Río Paují lay inside a vast petroleum concession of around 270,000 square kilometres obtained in 1909, under the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a>, by John Alen Tregelles for the Venezuela Development Company. In 1912 the land passed through the lawyer Rafael Max Valladares to the Caribbean Petroleum Company, founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in 1911 as a subsidiary of the General Asphalt Company. Exploration began in September 1912 under the geologist Ralph Arnold, whose final report recommended drilling at once in the Zumaque area near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mene-grande">Mene Grande</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The well stands in the Baralt Municipality of Zulia, a few kilometres from the shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a>. The company marked it MG-1, the first well of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mene-grande">Mene Grande</a> field, but it took its common name from the Zumaque hacienda, named after a bush called by the indigenous word zumaque. Drilling began in April 1914 on an anticline of La Estrella hill, in jungle that had to be cleared, by workers with no experience of oil who laboured in great heat without drinking water or medical care.</p><p>With a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> derrick and a hammer drill the crews struggled to control the reservoir's pressure and the well blew out, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> often did then. By 25 July 1914 it had reached 135 metres, and on 31 July it was declared commercially useful, flowing 264 barrels a day of 18° API crude.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In 1917 the company opened the San Lorenzo Refinery at San Timoteo, with a capacity of 8,000 barrels a day, the first of its kind in Venezuela. Mene Grande was also a pioneer of trade unionism: an oil union formed there in 1925 was vetoed by Gómez, and on 14 December 1936 the first large-scale oil strike in the country's history was called from Zumaque I and harshly repressed by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleazar-lopez-contreras">Eleazar López Contreras</a>. On 1 January 1976 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-andres-perez">Carlos Andrés Pérez</a> held the ceremonies of the oil industry's nationalisation at the well, which passed to Maraven, a subsidiary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petroleos-de-venezuela">Petróleos de Venezuela</a>. In 2014 it was still producing 18 to 20 barrels a day, mostly as a symbol.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zumaque I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Commercial production began with the <b>Zumaque I</b> well at Mene Grande in 1914, and the blowout of the Barroso II well near Cabimas in 1922 revealed the vast reserves of the Lake Maracaibo basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
